# 73. Gelfand's repeated characteristic-class discussion and metric-jet question.
talk(73,'1965-11-29','I. M. Gelfand','P000116','Chern forms, Bianchi identity and curvature jets',r"""After the residual Arnold sentence at the top of P000116, the dated Gelfand heading restarts the connection and Chern-form construction. The new mathematical content is the Bianchi/transgression argument and a precise question about relations among curvature and its covariant derivatives. The latter is compared with the metric-jet isomorphism theorem, not classified as an untouched problem. \citep{bottchern1965hermitian,jentsch2026jet}""")
item(73,'P000116','construction','Repeated moving-frame construction of Chern forms',r"""The connection, Cartan moving frame, \(K=d\theta-\theta\wedge\theta\), and \(\det(I+iK/2\pi)\) are repeated. This time the normalization is correct.""",r"""For a smooth complex bundle with connection in the notebook frame convention, the curvature matrix defines frame-invariant even-degree forms by \(c(E,D)=\det(I+iK/(2\pi))\). Each entry of \(K\) has degree two, so the determinant is taken in the commutative algebra of even scalar forms. The construction repeats items 1965-71.6--1965-71.9 and supplies an internal check on the reciprocal coefficient on P000112.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='characteristic classes and differential geometry')
item(73,'P000116','theorem','Bianchi identity and closed characteristic forms',r"""The displayed Bianchi identity is \(dK=-[K,\theta]\), described as the derivative of the curvature tensor.""",r"""For \(K=d\theta-\theta\wedge\theta\), direct exterior differentiation gives \(dK=\theta\wedge K-K\wedge\theta=-[K,\theta]\). Equivalently the covariant exterior derivative of the curvature is zero. Applying invariant polynomials and cyclicity of trace makes each component of the total Chern form closed.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='characteristic classes and differential geometry',development='annotated')
item(73,'P000116','method','Connection variation and transgression',r"""The notebook says that the form is independent of the connection and sketches expressing an integral by a boundary integral so that variation in the interior does not affect it.""",r"""For two connections \(D_0,D_1\) on a fixed complex bundle, the corresponding Chern forms differ by exact forms: \(c_j(E,D_1)-c_j(E,D_0)=dT_j(D_0,D_1)\). One derives this along the affine path \(D_t\), using Bianchi and invariant-polynomial differentiation. Consequently integrals over closed cycles are independent of the connection, but pointwise forms usually are not.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='characteristic classes and differential geometry',correct='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The transgression statement is the precise content of the sketched variation argument. For a manifold with boundary, the boundary term vanishes only under an appropriate fixed-boundary or relative condition.""")
item(73,'P000116','claim','Natural metric tensors from curvature and its derivatives',r"""Gelfand asks how to find all tensors formed from \(g_{\alpha\beta}\) and its derivatives, then says they are algebraic expressions in curvature and its covariant derivatives.""",r"""A finite-order natural tensor constructed from a Riemannian metric is determined in normal coordinates by the curvature tensor and finitely many covariant derivatives, equivariantly under changes of orthonormal frame. For polynomial natural tensors, invariant theory expresses them using tensor products, permutations and contractions with the metric and its inverse (and the volume tensor in an oriented setting). A general smooth natural invariant need only be a smooth function of such data; it need not be polynomial.""",['jentsch2026jet'],area='Riemannian geometry and invariant theory',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction=r"""Finite order, naturality and the distinction between polynomial and arbitrary smooth dependence are supplied explicitly. The jet-isomorphism part is checked in the opened primary source; no classification of unrestricted nonlocal constructions is asserted.""",fate=r"""The normal-coordinate replacement principle is classical. Jentsch's revised 2026 treatment gives an explicit modern jet-isomorphism formulation and credits the older theory; it is not cited as its first discovery. \citep{jentsch2026jet}""")
item(73,'P000116','question','Relations among covariant derivatives of curvature',r"""The definite question is \ru{Но какие алгебр. соотн.?}; the first Bianchi-type relations among covariant derivatives are mentioned.""",r"""Fix a Euclidean vector space \(V\) and an order \(k\). Which collections \((R,\nabla R,\ldots,\nabla^kR)\) at one point occur as the curvature jet of a smooth Riemannian metric with prescribed value at that point? The algebraic curvature symmetries, differentiated first and second Bianchi identities, and differentiated Ricci commutation identities are necessary; the metric-jet isomorphism theorem makes the corresponding algebraic-jet conditions sufficient. Nonlinear lower-order curvature terms in commutation identities cannot be discarded.""",['jentsch2026jet'],area='Riemannian geometry and invariant theory',cl='B',problem='GP-1965-M01',fate=r"""The exact finite-order realization formulation is covered by Definition 1 and Theorem 3 in the opened jet-isomorphism treatment, including the nonlinear commutation constraints. This does not date the classical theorem's first solution to 2026. \citep{jentsch2026jet}""",reconstruction=r"""A finite-order pointwise realization problem makes the notebook's all-relations question precise. It does not ask for a minimal presentation of scalar invariant rings in every dimension.""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, the finite-order curvature-jet realization question in this formulation is settled. Explicit reconstruction formulae continue to be useful, but no precise unsolved remainder is asserted.""")
item(73,'P000116','method','Normal-coordinate metric coefficients and their identities',r"""The source expands the metric without a linear term and says that all coefficients in a normal system are tensors; some Taylor indices are overwritten or repeated.""",r"""In geodesic normal coordinates about \(p\), \(g_{ij}(0)\) is the prescribed inner product and \(\partial_k g_{ij}(0)=0\). Changing the frame at \(p\) changes normal coordinates linearly, so their higher coefficient arrays are tensors for that restricted frame-change action. In one standard curvature-sign convention, \(g_{ij}(x)=g_{ij}(0)-\tfrac13R_{ikjl}(p)x^kx^l+O(|x|^3)\). General coordinate partial derivatives of the metric are not tensors. The normal-coordinate identity \(g_x(x,v)=g_0(x,v)\) provides the coefficient constraints.""",['jentsch2026jet'],area='Riemannian geometry and invariant theory',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The repeated dummy indices in the scan are not treated as a meaningful tensor formula. The precise normal-coordinate transformation group and curvature-sign convention are supplied.""")
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1965-M01',title='Algebraic realization of finite curvature jets',statement=r"""For a fixed dimension and order \(k\), characterize the pointwise arrays \((R,\nabla R,\ldots,\nabla^kR)\) realized by a Riemannian metric with prescribed zeroth-order inner product. The notebook asks for the algebraic relations among these tensors.""",occurrences=['1965-73.5'],status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, settled in the finite-order realization formulation by the metric-jet isomorphism theorem: curvature symmetries, differentiated Bianchi identities and Ricci commutation constraints define the realizable algebraic jets. No claim is made here about minimal invariant-ring presentations. \citep{jentsch2026jet}""",status_date='2026-11',refs=['jentsch2026jet']))
ev('Jentsch jet isomorphism theorem curvature derivatives identities','https://arxiv.org/pdf/1509.08269','read the result','Opened actual version 2 dated 13 January 2026, title The Jet Isomorphism Theorem of Riemannian Geometry. Definition 1 lists algebraic constraints; Theorem 3 gives isomorphism and realization. The result is expressly classical.','Definition 1 pp. 6-7; Theorem 2 p. 7; Theorem 3 pp. 10-11; introduction normal-coordinate coefficients','73')
