# 74. Arnold: bifurcation language, preparation, exact intersections.
talk(74,'1965-11-29','V. I. Arnold','P000117-P000118','Stability, preparation and intersections of invariant tori',r"""P000117 opens with Arnold on structural stability of flows and parameters, continues with planar desingularization and the Weierstrass--Malgrange division/preparation theorem, and P000118 introduces exact canonical transformations of \(T^n\times B^n\). The torus intersection claim is recognized as an early Arnold problem, with the notebook's graph argument recovered. Its \(2^n\) bound requires transversality; the exact-period condition also must be separated from an assumed Hamiltonian isotopy. \citep{malgrange1966ideals,chaperon1983questions,gong2023cuplength}""")
item(74,'P000117','definition','Structural stability of a flow and parameter-dependent families',r"""The notes write \(\dot x=f(x,C)\), a flow \(g^t\), and a conjugacy square; nearby systems should be related by a small change of the phase space.""",r"""For a vector field on a compact manifold, orbital structural stability means that all sufficiently close vector fields (in a specified \(C^r\) topology) are topologically equivalent by a homeomorphism sending oriented trajectories to oriented trajectories. Time-preserving conjugacy is stronger. A parameter family can cross nonstable systems even if its nearby parameter regions are structurally stable; it therefore requires bifurcation theory, not only classification of open stable classes.""",['arnold1968singularities'],area='dynamical systems',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='thematic only',reconstruction=r"""The equivalence category, topology and treatment of time are absent and are explicitly supplied as a conventional interpretation. The cited mapping exposition is contextual, not an exact theorem for flows.""",verification='indirect')
item(74,'P000117','claim','A centre and the unspecified degree of instability',r"""A number of \ru{когрубости} is proposed, and a centre is said to have infinite degree of instability; concentric orbits are drawn.""",r"""The elementary content is that a planar centre is not structurally stable in the unrestricted class of smooth planar vector fields: an arbitrarily small radial component changes the closed orbits to spirals. A numerical codimension or infinite-degree statement is not determined until the equivalence group, analytic or smooth category, and permitted family are fixed. In a Hamiltonian or reversible subclass the stability question changes.""",['arnold1968singularities'],area='dynamical systems',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',development='annotated',fate=r"""Singularity and bifurcation theory develop such codimension questions, but the precise centre claim in this notation is not identified. \citep{arnold1968singularities}""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, the unrestricted instability example is elementary; the notebook's numerical degree remains insufficiently specified. This is not converted into a current open problem.""")
item(74,'P000117','claim','Thom strata transferred from map germs to differential equations',r"""The display \(\vartheta=\vartheta^\infty\cup\bigcup_\gamma\vartheta^\gamma\) is followed by partly contradictory codimension annotations, finite-parameter descriptions, and a claim that points inside a stratum are diffeomorphic.""",r"""The notebook does not identify whether \(\vartheta\) consists of map germs, vector-field germs, or parameter families, or whether equivalence is smooth, orbital or topological. The Thom germ-classification statement for maps in item 1965-72.11 does not automatically give the asserted classification for differential equations. The claim that all one-parameter families lie in an infinite-codimension exceptional part is also uninterpretable without the missing quantifiers.""",['arnold1968singularities','mather1970topological'],area='dynamical systems',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low',fate=r"""The opened Thom--Mather sources clarify the map-theoretic model but do not justify the transfer in this damaged passage. \citep{arnold1968singularities,mather1970topological}""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, no precise residual theorem or conjecture is extracted from these conflicting annotations. F records source indeterminacy, not absence of research.""")
item(74,'P000117','method','Planar desingularization by point blow-ups',r"""Bendixson, \emph{Acta Mathematica} 1901, is cited for finite \(\sigma\)-processes applied to \(dy/dx=P/Q\), with a chart substitution \(y/x\), and a model \(x^pdy/dx=\lambda y+P(x,y)\).""",r"""For a germ of an analytic planar foliation with an isolated singularity, a point blow-up replaces the origin by its space of tangent directions; one affine chart has \(y=xu\). After removing common exceptional factors, finite reduction leads to elementary singularities, including nondegenerate singularities and saddle-nodes. The displayed equation is a local schematic model, not a complete normal form without conditions on \(p,\lambda\) and the remainder. The complex projective formulation in the notebook goes beyond the directly checked real planar discussion in Bendixson.""",['bendixson1901courbes'],area='dynamical systems and desingularization',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',confidence='medium',fate=r"""Bendixson's primary paper is correctly identified and its repeated substitutions and local cases are read. The full complex-foliation reduction statement is a modern comparison, not an exact theorem attributed to those pages. \citep{bendixson1901courbes}""",reconstruction=r"""Isolated singularity, removal of common factors and elementary local types are supplied. No uniqueness or analytic linearization of all reduced singularities is claimed.""")
item(74,'P000117','question','The unspecified three-variable reduction problem',r"""Immediately after the planar statement the notes say \ru{Для 3-х пер. неизв.} and refer to simple singularities on a birationally equivalent manifold.""",r"""To state a three-dimensional resolution problem one must specify the rank or codimension of the foliation, the real or complex category, the allowed centres and whether weighted blow-ups are permitted, and the terminal class of singularities. The notebook supplies none of these. A bare analogy with the planar equation does not identify a single modern conjecture.""",['bendixson1901courbes'],area='dynamical systems and desingularization',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',fate=r"""The named 1901 source is planar. The historical three-variable remark cannot be equated without hypotheses to any one later resolution theorem or remaining higher-dimensional problem. \citep{bendixson1901courbes}""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, no current open-status claim is made for this underspecified sentence. It is not added to the problem index.""")
item(74,'P000117','definition','Distinguished Weierstrass polynomials',r"""The notes write \(P(y)=y^n+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}y^ia_i(x,z)\), with \(a_i(0,0)=0\); the constant coefficient is absent.""",r"""For parameter variables \(u=(x,z)\), a distinguished polynomial is \(P(y,u)=y^n+\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}a_i(u)y^i\), where each \(a_i(0)=0\). The coefficients lie in the relevant analytic, formal or smooth germ ring. The term \(a_0\) is essential; omitting it forces divisibility by \(y\) and does not describe a general distinguished polynomial.""",['malgrange1966ideals'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='false as written',development='annotated')
item(74,'P000117','theorem','Analytic and formal Weierstrass division',r"""The stated division is \(f=PQ+\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}b_i(u)y^i\), with coefficients and quotient of the same class; the quotient by \((P)\) is finite over the parameter ring.""",r"""If \(P\) is a distinguished polynomial of degree \(n\), every analytic germ \(f(y,u)\) has a unique division \(f=PQ+R\), where \(Q\) is analytic and \(R=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}b_i(u)y^i\) has analytic coefficients. The analogous formal statement holds over characteristic-zero formal power-series rings. The analytic or formal quotient by \((P)\) is free of rank \(n\) over the parameter germ ring, with basis \(1,y,\ldots,y^{n-1}\). This is finite module rank, not finite dimension over the scalar field when parameters remain.""",['malgrange1966ideals'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(74,'P000117','theorem','Smooth division and its loss of uniqueness',r"""Malgrange's \(C^\infty\) analogue is said to hold but without uniqueness.""",r"""For a smooth distinguished polynomial near the origin and a smooth germ \(f\), smooth division produces a smooth quotient and a remainder polynomial in \(y\) of degree below \(n\), with smooth parameter coefficients. Such a representation need not be unique. The quotient module is finitely generated over the parameter ring; it must not automatically be called free of rank \(n\), because smooth flat terms can create relations.""",['malgrange1966ideals'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',fate=r"""Malgrange's primary monograph contains the preparation/division theorem and discusses nonunique remainders; its lectures were delivered in 1964 and foreword dated 1965. The 1966 imprint is therefore not evidence of a post-talk discovery. \citep{malgrange1966ideals}""",reconstruction=r"""Finite generation, rather than freeness, is the statement justified in the smooth category.""")
item(74,'P000117','method','Interpolation when the roots are real',r"""The notes propose Lagrange interpolation when the polynomial has \(n\) real roots, with a warning about smooth dependence.""",r"""For distinct real roots, the remainder is determined by the values of \(f\) at the roots through Lagrange interpolation. At repeated real roots, Hermite interpolation prescribes derivatives to the corresponding multiplicities. Divided-difference formulae explain why the remainder has smooth symmetric dependence when roots coalesce; the naive formula with denominators of root differences requires this limiting argument. In the general non-real-root case smooth uniqueness fails.""",['malgrange1966ideals'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""Multiplicity and the divided-difference passage, not merely distinct-root interpolation, supply the omitted smoothness issue.""")
item(74,'P000117','claim','The flat-function counterexample to unique division',r"""For a smooth function \(f(x)\) flat at zero, the notes divide by \(y^2+x^2\) in two ways and attribute smoothness of the nontrivial quotient to a Lojasiewicz argument.""",r"""Let \(f\in C^\infty(\mathbb R)\) satisfy \(f^{(j)}(0)=0\) for every \(j\). Put \(q(x,y)=f(x)/(x^2+y^2)\) off the origin and \(q(0,0)=0\). Then \(q\) is smooth and flat: every derivative is bounded by a finite sum of \(|f^{(j)}(x)|\,(x^2+y^2)^{-N}\), and flatness absorbs each denominator. Thus \(f=(x^2+y^2)\cdot0+f\) and \(f=(x^2+y^2)q+0\) are distinct smooth divisions when \(f\ne0\).""",['malgrange1966ideals'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The derivative estimate is this task's explicit justification of the source example; no quantitative Lojasiewicz theorem is needed for this denominator.""")
item(74,'P000118','theorem','Preparation of a germ regular in one variable',r"""If \(f(0,0)=0\) and some \(\partial_y^n f(0,0)\ne0\), the notes write \(f=P Q\), with distinguished \(P\) and \(Q(0,0)\ne0\); nonflatness is said to allow this.""",r"""If \(f\) has exact finite order \(n\) on the chosen \(y\)-axis, meaning \(\partial_y^j f(0,0)=0\) for \(j<n\) and \(\partial_y^n f(0,0)\ne0\), then \(f=U(y,u)(y^n+\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}a_i(u)y^i)\), with \(U(0,0)\ne0\) and \(a_i(0)=0\), in the analytic, formal or smooth setting. Analytic/formal preparation is unique in its normalized form; smooth preparation need not be. A nonflat germ admits a regular direction after a suitable linear coordinate change, not necessarily along the originally chosen axis.""",['malgrange1966ideals'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(74,'P000118','claim','An essential circle meets its area-preserving image',r"""An area-preserving annulus diffeomorphism homotopic to the identity is said to satisfy \(\#(A\gamma\cap\gamma)\ge2\) for every closed curve surrounding the hole.""",r"""Let \(A\) be an area-preserving diffeomorphism of a compact annulus, isotopic to the identity, and let \(\gamma\) be an embedded essential circle in its interior. If \(A\gamma\) and \(\gamma\) meet transversely, they have at least two intersections. Were they disjoint, one would lie strictly on one side of the other and the enclosed annular region would contradict equality of the area between each curve and the same boundary component. A transverse intersection count between isotopic essential circles is even.""",['chaperon1983questions'],area='symplectic geometry',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""Embeddedness, a finite-area annulus and transversality give a clean sufficient version of the elementary claim. Homotopy alone for an arbitrary closed, possibly self-intersecting curve does not supply this argument.""")
item(74,'P000118','definition','Globally canonical transformations and exactness',r"""On \(T^n\times B^n\), the notes impose \(\oint_{A\gamma}p\,dq=\oint_\gamma p\,dq\) for closed loops, calling this globally canonical.""",r"""With \(\lambda=\sum p_i\,dq_i\), equality of these integrals for every closed loop is equivalent to \(A^*\lambda-\lambda=dS\) for a global smooth function \(S\), assuming a connected domain. Differentiating gives \(A^*d\lambda=d\lambda\), so \(A\) is exact symplectic. Ordinary symplecticity only makes the difference closed. A compactly supported Hamiltonian isotopy has exact time-one map; an exact endpoint homotopic to the identity should not be silently substituted for a specified Hamiltonian isotopy without an additional argument.""",['chaperon1983questions'],area='symplectic geometry',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The de Rham period argument makes the globally canonical condition precise. This entry deliberately does not assert that all endpoint-exact maps in the notebook's unspecified domain lie in the Hamiltonian component.""")
item(74,'P000118','conjecture','Arnold torus-intersection bound and the missing transversality',r"""For the zero torus \(T=\{p=0\}\), the notebook labels \(\#(AT\cap T)\ge2^n\) a theorem, but proves only the graphical case under an additional assumption. No transversality is written.""",r"""A precise established version is: if \(\phi\) is a compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of \(T^*T^n\) and \(\phi(T^n)\) meets the zero section transversely, then \(\#(\phi(T^n)\cap T^n)\ge\sum_j b_j(T^n;\mathbb F_2)=2^n\). Without transversality the universal cup-length lower bound is \(n+1\). The literal \(2^n\) assertion is false already for \(n=2\): \(S(x,y)=\sin x+\sin y-\sin(x+y)\) has exactly three critical points, and a small graph of \(dS\) is a Hamiltonian image of the zero section.""",['chaperon1983questions','auroux2026floer','gong2023cuplength'],area='symplectic geometry',cl='B',correct='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',problem='GP-1965-M02',fate=r"""Chaperon's 1983 exposition records the torus progress; the transverse Floer bound and the cotangent-bundle nontransverse cup-length theorem are checked in the opened later sources. This solves the reconstructed Hamiltonian versions, not an unverified equivalence of all endpoint-exact hypotheses. \citep{chaperon1983questions,auroux2026floer,gong2023cuplength}""",reconstruction=r"""Hamiltonian isotopy, compact support and transverse intersection are sufficient modern hypotheses. The counterexample and its cutoff Hamiltonian realization are independently proved in the accompanying report. Broader variants of the Arnold conjecture are not imported as new notebook problems.""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, the stated transverse Hamiltonian torus bound and nontransverse cup-length bound are established. The literal unqualified bound fails. This occurrence has no claimed new C/D remainder.""")
item(74,'P000118','method','The graph argument reduces intersections to Morse critical points',r"""The notes assume \(AT\) can be written \(p=p(q)\) and finish by invoking Morse theory on the torus.""",r"""If an exact Lagrangian image of the zero section is the graph of a one-form \(\alpha\) on \(T^n\), then \(\alpha=dS\). Its intersections with the zero section are exactly \(\operatorname{Crit}(S)\), and they are transverse precisely when \(S\) is Morse. Morse inequalities imply at least \(2^n\) such points. For a general smooth \(S\), Lusternik--Schnirelmann cup length gives at least \(n+1\) distinct critical points; it does not imply \(2^n\).""",['chaperon1983questions','gong2023cuplength'],area='symplectic geometry',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',problem='GP-1965-M02',reconstruction=r"""The graph assumption and exact periods recover a generating function. The Morse nondegeneracy condition is missing from the notebook and is indispensable to its numerical conclusion.""")
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1965-M02',title='Intersections of the zero torus with its Hamiltonian image',statement=r"""For the zero section \(T^n\subset T^*T^n\), bound the number of intersections with its image under a compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphism. The transverse formulation asks for at least \(2^n\) intersections; in the possibly degenerate formulation the cup-length lower bound is \(n+1\). The notebook writes only exact periods and an unqualified \(2^n\), so these additional hypotheses are explicitly reconstructed.""",occurrences=['1965-74.14','1965-74.15'],status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, both stated Hamiltonian bounds are established. The unqualified \(2^n\) version is false: a graph of the differential of \(\sin x+\sin y-\sin(x+y)\) on \(T^2\) has three intersections. No open general Arnold-conjecture variant is inferred from this record. \citep{chaperon1983questions,auroux2026floer,gong2023cuplength}""",status_date='2026-11',refs=['chaperon1983questions','auroux2026floer','gong2023cuplength']))
