# 75. Chern connections, Watts, Levinson and Whitney.
talk(75,'1965-12-06','I. M. Gelfand','P000119-P000121','Hermitian connections, cohomology from rings, and analytic canonical forms',r"""The dated Gelfand heading begins P000119. The discussion derives the Chern connection and its curvature, then reports Watts's October 1965 algebraic model for Alexander--Spanier cohomology. The Levinson and Whitney discussion on the upper part of P000121 has no intervening speaker heading and is assigned to this record by source sequence; Arnold's new heading begins the fluid discussion below it. The repeated Chern-form argument contains a genuine graded-sign error, and the Whitney example has five factors although the prose twice counts four surfaces. \citep{bottchern1965hermitian,watts1965alexander,lladser2007uniqueness,arnold1968singularities}""")
item(75,'P000119','definition','Connections and bundle-valued differential forms',r"""\(D:\Gamma(E)\to\Gamma(T^*X\otimes E)\), \(D(fs)=df\,s+fDs\), and \(A(X,E)=A(X)\otimes_{A^0(X)}\Gamma(E)\).""",r"""For a smooth finite-rank complex bundle \(E\to X\), a connection is a complex-linear differential operator satisfying the displayed Leibniz identity. It extends to \(\Omega^q(X,E)\) by \(D(\alpha\otimes s)=d\alpha\otimes s+(-1)^q\alpha\wedge Ds\). Local tensor expressions give the bundle-valued form sheaf; on a compact smooth manifold the global tensor description follows from finite projectivity of \(\Gamma(E)\).""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The graded extension and a sufficient compactness hypothesis for the global algebraic tensor presentation are explicit. No completed tensor product is intended.""")
item(75,'P000119','construction','Curvature and the connection-independent Chern classes',r"""In the row-frame convention \(Ds_i=\sum_j\theta_{ij}s_j\), \(K=d\theta-\theta\wedge\theta\) and \(C(E,D)=\det(1+iK/(2\pi))\); the next line says that this does not depend on the connection.""",r"""With this convention the curvature matrix is \(K=d\theta-\theta\wedge\theta\). The even-degree entries commute as scalar forms, so the determinant defines closed forms \(c_j(E,D)\). Their de Rham cohomology classes are independent of \(D\); the differential forms themselves generally vary by exact transgression terms. In a column-frame convention the associated signs and matrix placement change together.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The notebook's independence claim is repaired at the level of cohomology classes, not by changing its faithfully transcribed curvature sign.""")
item(75,'P000119','definition','Type decomposition on a holomorphic bundle',r"""\(d=d'+d''\), \(D=D'+D''\), with bidegrees \((1,0)\) and \((0,1)\); the notes call these two connections.""",r"""On a complex manifold, \(d=\partial+\bar\partial\) and \(\Omega^k=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}\Omega^{p,q}\). Any complex connection splits into operators \(D'\) and \(D''\) of the corresponding bidegrees. They are partial connections, with Leibniz terms \(\partial f\) and \(\bar\partial f\), rather than two separate full connections. The local generators of scalar \((p,q)\)-forms use holomorphic coordinate functions and their conjugates; the \(f_i\) in that display are not sections of an arbitrary vector bundle.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The word connection in the split display is interpreted as partial connection. The reference to analytic sections in the form generators is repaired to local holomorphic scalar coordinates.""")
item(75,'P000119','theorem','Existence and uniqueness of the Chern connection',r"""The headline says that a Hermitian metric determines a unique compatible connection; the numbered conditions additionally require \(D''s=0\) for holomorphic \(s\).""",r"""On a holomorphic vector bundle \(E\) with a smooth positive-definite Hermitian metric \(h\), there is a unique connection preserving \(h\) and satisfying \(D''=\bar\partial_E\). The metric alone allows many compatible connections; compatibility with the specified holomorphic structure supplies the uniqueness condition. A local holomorphic frame then has connection matrix of type \((1,0)\).""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The two numbered conditions, read together, recover the correct theorem despite the abbreviated headline.""")
item(75,'P000119','definition','Hermitian pairing of bundle-valued forms',r"""The displayed formula is \(\langle s\otimes\alpha,s'\otimes\beta\rangle=\langle s,s'\rangle\alpha\wedge\beta\), with no visible conjugation over \(\beta\).""",r"""With the Hermitian metric linear in its first argument, the natural sesquilinear form-valued pairing is \(h(s\otimes\alpha,s'\otimes\beta)=h(s,s')\alpha\wedge\overline\beta\). The conjugation is required for compatibility with moving a complex scalar between the section and form factors. For homogeneous bundle-valued forms, the differential compatibility formula has the usual graded sign.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The scan and Russian transcription both omit the bar. It is a mathematical correction in this annotation, not evidence that the transcription lost a visible stroke.""")
item(75,'P000119-P000120','construction','The metric matrix determines the Chern connection matrix',r"""\(N_{ij}=\langle s_i,s_j\rangle\), \(dN=\theta N+N\overline\theta^t\), hence \(d'N=\theta N\) and \(\theta=d'N\,N^{-1}\).""",r"""For a local holomorphic row frame and a metric linear in the first argument, \(N\) is a positive Hermitian matrix. Metric compatibility gives \(dN=\theta N+N\overline\theta^t\), and the \((1,0)\)-part yields the uniquely determined matrix \(\theta=(\partial N)N^{-1}\). This matrix has the connection transformation law under holomorphic frame changes.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes')
item(75,'P000120','claim','The Maurer--Cartan identity and the mistaken minus sign',r"""The notes first write \(d'\theta=\theta\wedge\theta\), then question it, and differentiate \(\theta=d'N\,N^{-1}\) with a minus sign on the resulting product.""",r"""The positive identity is correct: \[\partial\theta=-\partial N\wedge\partial(N^{-1})=(\partial N)N^{-1}\wedge(\partial N)N^{-1}=\theta\wedge\theta.\] Both the graded Leibniz sign from the one-form \(\partial N\) and the sign in \(\partial N^{-1}=-N^{-1}(\partial N)N^{-1}\) are needed; they cancel.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""\ours{} This graded calculation identifies exactly the missing sign. The minus in the handwritten differentiated line is genuine, so it is retained in the source apparatus.""")
item(75,'P000120','theorem','Curvature of type (1,1), Bianchi, and Chern-form types',r"""\(K=d''\theta\), \(d''K=0\), \(d'K=-[K,\theta]\); the notes conclude that all characteristic classes have type \((p,p)\).""",r"""For the Chern connection, \(K=\bar\partial\theta\) has type \((1,1)\), \(\bar\partial K=0\), and \(\partial K=-[K,\theta]\), where the bracket is the graded matrix commutator. Consequently \(c_j(E,h)\) has type \((j,j)\), is closed, and represents the de Rham Chern class. On a general non-Kahler manifold this statement concerns a representative; it does not presume a Hodge decomposition of all de Rham cohomology.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The last phrase is restricted to the Chern forms of the holomorphic bundle and is separated from a global Hodge-decomposition assertion.""")
item(75,'P000120','construction','Curvature of a Hermitian holomorphic line bundle',r"""In rank one, \(N(x)>0\), \(\theta=d'\log N\) and \(K=d''d'\log N\); the characteristic class is the same up to a factor.""",r"""In a local nonvanishing holomorphic section \(s\), put \(N=h(s,s)\). Then \(K=\bar\partial\partial\log N\), and \(c_1(E,h)=\frac{i}{2\pi}\bar\partial\partial\log N\). Under holomorphic rescaling of \(s\), the extra logarithm is pluriharmonic, so the curvature glues globally. The order \(\bar\partial\partial\) matches the notebook's row-frame convention.""",['bottchern1965hermitian'],area='complex geometry and characteristic classes')
item(75,'P000120','construction','Watts tensor complex and its differential',r"""\(F^0=\Lambda\), \(F^p=\Lambda\otimes_KF^{p-1}\), insertion maps \(d_i\) add a unit, and \(d^p=\sum(-1)^id_i\). The prose calls \(F^p\) functions of \(p\) variables.""",r"""For a commutative unital \(K\)-algebra \(\Lambda\), set \(F^p=\Lambda^{\otimes_K(p+1)}\). For \(0\le i\le p+1\), let \(d_i:F^p\to F^{p+1}\) insert \(1\) in position \(i\), and put \(d^p=\sum_{i=0}^{p+1}(-1)^id_i\). The cosimplicial insertion identities imply \(d^{p+1}d^p=0\). For a function algebra, a simple tensor is a function of \(p+1\) variables, not \(p\); no completion to all continuous functions on the product is part of this algebraic definition.""",['watts1965alexander'],area='algebraic topology and function algebras',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""Commutativity, a unit, the limits of the alternating sum, and the variable count are supplied or corrected from Watts's exact construction.""")
item(75,'P000120','definition','Algebraic germs along the diagonal',r"""\(\mu(f_0\otimes\cdots\otimes f_p)=f_0\cdots f_p\); for a maximal ideal \(M\subset\Lambda\), an element vanishes near \(M\) if it dies upon localization at \(\mu^{-1}M\).""",r"""The multiplication map \(\mu_p:F^p\to\Lambda\) is a surjective algebra homomorphism, so \(\mu_p^{-1}M\) is maximal. An element \(f\in F^p\) maps to zero in \((F^p)_{\mu_p^{-1}M}\) exactly if there exists \(g\notin\mu_p^{-1}M\) with \(gf=0\). For \(\Lambda=C(X,\mathbb R)\) and compact Hausdorff \(X\), maximal ideals correspond to points and \(\mu\) is restriction to the diagonal.""",['watts1965alexander'],area='algebraic topology and function algebras',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The point interpretation is only asserted for the specified continuous real-function algebra; arbitrary maximal ideals need not have this topological interpretation.""")
item(75,'P000120','construction','The quotient by functions locally zero at the diagonal',r"""\(N^p\) consists of elements vanishing in a strip around the diagonal; \(d^pN^p\subset N^{p+1}\), and the notes call the resulting \(H^p\) homology.""",r"""Let \(N^p=\bigcap_{M\in\operatorname{Max}\Lambda}\ker(F^p\to(F^p)_{\mu_p^{-1}M})\). The insertion differential preserves this family, so \(F^\bullet/N^\bullet\) is a cochain complex. Its degree-\(p\) group is cohomology, \(H^p=\ker d^p/\operatorname{im}d^{p-1}\). For a compact function space, the local condition is vanishing on some neighborhood of the whole diagonal.""",['watts1965alexander'],area='algebraic topology and function algebras',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The missing algebraic definition of the diagonal neighborhood is filled by the intersection of localization kernels; the grading distinguishes cohomology from the source's informal homology label.""")
item(75,'P000120','theorem','Recovering Alexander--Spanier cohomology from C(X)',r"""The final parenthesis says that the groups agree with ordinary Alexander--Spanier cohomology; Watts, PNAS 54(4), October 1965, is named.""",r"""For compact Hausdorff \(X\), the preceding construction with \(K=\mathbb R\) and \(\Lambda=C(X,\mathbb R)\) computes Alexander--Spanier cohomology \(H^p(X;\mathbb R)\). Thus this cohomology is determined functorially by the unital real algebra of continuous real functions. The statement does not identify the construction for every abstract algebra with the cohomology of an unspecified spectrum.""",['watts1965alexander'],area='algebraic topology and function algebras',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',fate=r"""The exact two-page source was published in PNAS 54(4), 1027--1028, in October 1965 and is read in full. The talk is reporting that result, not posing a new cohomology problem. \citep{watts1965alexander}""")
item(75,'P000121','theorem','Levinson polynomial form in one distinguished variable',r"""The notes cite Duke 1961, pp. 345--353, and assume finite order \(n\) in \(y\). An analytic coordinate change makes \(f\) a polynomial in the new \(y\), with analytic coefficients in the other variables.""",r"""Let \(f(y,x)\) be an analytic germ with \(\partial_y^j f(0,0)=0\) for \(j<n\) and \(\partial_y^n f(0,0)\ne0\). There are an analytic local coordinate \(u=u(y,x)\), with \(\partial_yu(0,0)\ne0\), and analytic \(a_j(x)\) such that \(f(y,x)=\sum_{j=0}^{n}a_j(x)u(y,x)^j\), where \(a_n(0)\ne0\) and \(a_j(0)=0\) for \(j<n\). This is polynomialization after a coordinate change, not merely Weierstrass preparation with an extra unit factor.""",['levinson1961canonical','lladser2007uniqueness'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',match='exact',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',fate=r"""Levinson's 1961 bibliographic identification agrees with the notebook. The exact canonical-form statement is read in Lladser's later primary research paper, equation (2), which cites the 1960 announcement; the original 1961 proof was not obtained. \citep{levinson1961canonical,lladser2007uniqueness}""",reconstruction=r"""Analytic germs are understood over \(\mathbb C\), or in the real-analytic version with real data. The inverse-function condition on the new coordinate makes the notebook's word diffeomorphism precise.""")
item(75,'P000121','claim','Polynomialization of analytic functions of two variables',r"""The next line says that with two variables one can make an actual polynomial, without further qualifications.""",r"""A safe precise version is that an analytic function germ with an isolated critical point is analytically right-equivalent to a polynomial germ, by finite determinacy; this includes the corresponding two-variable case. Levinson's cited two-variable result has additional algebraic hypotheses in its original formulation. The unrestricted wording in this line is not used to infer that every analytic germ in any dimension is polynomializable, or to identify a new unresolved classification problem.""",['levinson1960polynomial','lladser2007uniqueness','arnold1968singularities'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',fate=r"""The pre-talk Levinson publication is explicitly identified by later primary authors. Arnold's exposition contrasts two variables with Whitney's nonisolated three-variable obstruction. The original Levinson proof was not accessed, and no claim is made to have verified its full maximum generality. \citep{levinson1960polynomial,lladser2007uniqueness,arnold1968singularities}""",reconstruction=r"""The isolated-critical-point condition is a sufficient reconstruction, not a recovered handwritten hypothesis. The still broader two-variable formulation remains a limitation of this literature check, not a claim of current openness.""")
item(75,'P000121','construction','Whitney example with five branches',r"""The example is \(xy(y-x)(y-xz)(x\varphi(z)-y)\), with \(\varphi(0)=0\) and \(\varphi\) transcendental; the notes say four surfaces but later use the two quadruples \((1,2,3,4)\) and \((1,2,3,5)\).""",r"""Choose an analytic germ \(\varphi\) algebraically transcendental over \(\mathbb C(z)\), for example \(e^z-1\). The five factors define five smooth surface germs meeting along the \(z\)-axis, with generically distinct tangent planes away from exceptional axis points. This is the Whitney-type construction of an analytic three-variable germ whose zero-set geometry obstructs polynomialization by a smooth local diffeomorphism. Its critical locus is nonisolated, so it does not contradict finite determinacy at isolated critical points.""",['arnold1968singularities'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',confidence='medium',fate=r"""Arnold's 1968 exposition, printed p. 39, reproduces Whitney's five-factor counterexample with an exponential factor and attributes it to Whitney's 1965 work. The notebook's parameterized variant has the same mechanism, but its overwritten factors prevent a letter-for-letter exact match. \citep{arnold1968singularities}""",reconstruction=r"""\ours{} Five, rather than four, branches are forced by the product and by the two listed quadruples. Algebraic transcendence, rather than merely being a nonpolynomial analytic function, is the property needed by the cross-ratio argument.""")
item(75,'P000121','method','The pair of cross-ratios obstructs algebraic realization',r"""The anharmonic ratio of tangent planes through a common line is called a diffeomorphism invariant. A curve in the two cross-ratio coordinates is transcendental for the example but would be algebraic for a polynomial.""",r"""Modulo the common tangent line, five tangent planes give five points of a projective line. A local diffeomorphism acts by one projective linear transformation on this normal plane, hence preserves the two cross-ratios formed using the first three marked planes. After fixed rational changes of cross-ratio coordinates, the resulting parameterized curve is \(z\mapsto(z,\varphi(z))\). A polynomial configuration yields an algebraic cross-ratio relation along its algebraic multiple-branch locus; choosing \(\varphi\) algebraically transcendental contradicts that relation. Permuting the finitely many branches only applies finitely many rational coordinate changes and does not remove the obstruction.""",['arnold1968singularities'],area='singularity theory and local algebra',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The argument is local on the open axis stratum with distinct planes; it does not assume that the coordinate change itself is analytic. The five-branch count and algebraic-transcendence requirement make the sketch precise.""")
