# 76. Stationary incompressible Euler flow.
talk(76,'1965-12-06','V. I. Arnold','P000121-P000122','The topology of analytic stationary fluid flows',r"""Arnold's underlined heading on the lower part of P000121 begins this record, which ends immediately before the 13 December heading on P000122. It presents the commuting velocity and vorticity fields and the decomposition of an analytic steady flow by regular Bernoulli levels. Arnold's 1966 full paper gives an exact expanded match, explicitly referring to his earlier 1965 announcement. The notebook's separate compact-orbit lemma is too broad and is disproved here; individual dense streamlines must also be distinguished from the compact joint two-dimensional orbit. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""")
item(76,'P000121','definition','Vorticity and its transport in an ideal incompressible flow',r"""The velocity is tangent to the boundary, its vorticity is \(\tau=\operatorname{rot}v\), and a partly diagrammatic evolution equation has an unspecified sign before a Poisson bracket.""",r"""For a smooth inviscid incompressible velocity \(v\) in an oriented Euclidean three-dimensional domain, put \(\omega=\nabla\times v\). With \([v,w]=(v\cdot\nabla)w-(w\cdot\nabla)v\), the unforced Euler equation implies \(\partial_t\omega+[v,\omega]=0\). The boundary condition for an impermeable wall is \(v\cdot n=0\). Reversing the vector-field bracket convention reverses the displayed bracket sign.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""A definite modern bracket convention resolves the handwritten \(\pm\) without guessing which convention was intended in that line.""")
item(76,'P000121','claim','The curl of a cross product and the bracket sign',r"""\(\operatorname{rot}(a\ast b)=[a\ b]+a\operatorname{div}b-b\operatorname{div}a\), with \(\ast\) identified as vector product.""",r"""For the standard vector-field bracket used in item 1965-76.1, \[\nabla\times(a\times b)=-[a,b]+a\,\nabla\cdot b-b\,\nabla\cdot a.\] Thus the notebook's plus sign is consistent with its opposite bracket convention. In particular, commuting divergence-free fields have curl-free cross product regardless of the convention.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""\ours{} Component expansion checks the sign. This is a convention reconciliation, not a transcription correction.""")
item(76,'P000121-P000122','theorem','Steady Euler flow, commuting vorticity, and global pressure',r"""The three conditions are \(\operatorname{div}v=0\), \([v,\operatorname{rot}v]=0\), and tangency to the boundary. A margin note says that this is the right definition in a simply connected domain.""",r"""A smooth steady unforced Euler solution satisfies \((v\cdot\nabla)v=-\nabla p\), so \(v\times\omega=\nabla B\) with \(B=p+|v|^2/2\), and therefore \([v,\omega]=0\). Conversely, for divergence-free \(v\), commutation implies that \((v\times\omega)^\flat\) is closed. A global Bernoulli function, hence pressure, follows when \(H^1(D;\mathbb R)=0\), or more generally when all periods of that closed one-form vanish. Tangency supplies the impermeable boundary condition but does not alone remove a cohomological obstruction.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The handwritten simply-connected qualification is mathematically substantive and is retained; its sufficient cohomological generalization is made explicit.""")
item(76,'P000121-P000122','theorem','Arnold analytic decomposition of a stationary flow',r"""For an analytic stationary velocity in a bounded domain, not everywhere collinear with its curl, finitely many analytic pieces separate cells with torus windings or cylindrical rotations.""",r"""Let \(D\) be a connected compact three-dimensional real-analytic Riemannian domain with real-analytic boundary, and let \(v\) be a real-analytic stationary incompressible Euler field tangent to the boundary, analytic up to it. Assume \(v\times\operatorname{curl}v\) is not identically zero. Outside a proper compact analytic exceptional set, regular Bernoulli level components are embedded tori or annuli. On a torus the streamlines are conjugate to a constant linear flow and hence are closed or dense; on an annulus they are closed. The critical values of the Bernoulli function, including its boundary restriction, determine finitely many regular cells.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',fate=r"""Theorem 7 in Arnold's full 1966 paper, printed pp. 346--348, is an exact expanded match and cites his 1965 C. R. Acad. Sci. note, volume 261, p. 17. The result was therefore already announced before this December talk. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""",reconstruction=r"""Compactness, analyticity through the boundary, the global Bernoulli function, and the exceptional critical-level set are explicit. Boundedness and interior analyticity alone do not give the stated finiteness.""")
item(76,'P000122','construction','A common first integral for two commuting divergence-free fields',r"""The compact-orbit proof writes \(\operatorname{rot}(a\ast b)=0\), then calls \(a\times b\) a differential and identifies orbits with level surfaces.""",r"""On an oriented volume three-manifold \((M,\mu)\), if \(a,b\) preserve \(\mu\) and commute, the one-form \(\alpha=\iota_b\iota_a\mu\) is closed. If \(H^1(M;\mathbb R)=0\), then \(\alpha=dF\). Both fields are tangent to the levels of \(F\). Where they are independent, \(dF\ne0\), and their joint orbit is open in the connected regular level component. This last step does not identify a singular-level orbit with an entire closed level set.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""\ours{} Cartan's identity gives \(d\alpha=\mathcal L_b\iota_a\mu-\iota_b\mathcal L_a\mu=0\). Regularity and rank two are the missing steps in the notebook's orbit-to-level inference.""")
item(76,'P000122','theorem','A compact regular joint orbit is a torus',r"""The notes say that compact orbits without boundary are tori, with the one-parameter velocity action giving a winding.""",r"""Let \(C\) be a compact connected regular level surface without boundary on which the two commuting fields \(a,b\) are everywhere independent and tangent. Their complete flows define a locally free \(\mathbb R^2\)-action. Every orbit is open; connectedness makes the action transitive, so \(C\simeq\mathbb R^2/\Lambda\) for a discrete cocompact subgroup \(\Lambda\), necessarily a rank-two lattice. Thus \(C\simeq T^2\), and either field is a constant linear flow in these action coordinates. Its individual streamline may be dense and nonclosed although the joint \(\mathbb R^2\)-orbit is compact.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""\ours{} The action-and-lattice argument supplies the rank and completeness qualifications. Compact joint orbits are not confused with compact or closed individual streamlines.""")
item(76,'P000122','claim','The assertion that every joint orbit is compact is false',r"""The separately labelled mathematical theorem assumes \(b_1=0\), a finite or compact manifold, and two commuting divergence-free fields, and concludes that their orbits are compact.""",r"""These hypotheses do not force compact joint orbits. On the round \(S^3\subset\mathbb C^2\), let \(a(z_1,z_2)=(iz_1,i\alpha z_2)\), with \(\alpha\notin\mathbb Q\), and let \(b=0\). Both fields are smooth, complete and divergence-free, they commute, and \(H^1(S^3;\mathbb R)=0\). Through a point with \(z_1z_2\ne0\), the joint \(\mathbb R^2\)-orbit equals the irrational \(a\)-orbit and is dense but nonclosed in a Clifford torus, hence noncompact. The common first integral here is constant; its level set is not the orbit.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',fate=r"""Arnold's published proof only uses compact regular Bernoulli components with two independent tangent fields, as in item 1965-76.6; it does not state this unrestricted compact-orbit lemma. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""",reconstruction=r"""\ours{} The explicit counterexample isolates the missing rank/regular-level hypothesis. The term joint orbit is used deliberately: the failure is in the asserted \(\mathbb R^2\)-orbit theorem itself.""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, the literal standalone assertion is disproved by the displayed example; the regular-level torus theorem is established. No open compact-orbit problem is extracted.""")
item(76,'P000122','claim','Boundary components give annuli with closed streamlines',r"""The notes say that noncompact orbits are cylinders and illustrate fluid between rotating coaxial cylinders, with neighboring rings turning in opposite directions.""",r"""For a compact regular Bernoulli level component \(C\) meeting the boundary transversely in Arnold's setting, each boundary component is a closed velocity orbit. Commutation and independence propagate its period through \(C\), and \(C\) is an annulus with closed streamlines. It is not a theorem that every noncompact two-dimensional orbit of an \(\mathbb R^2\)-action is a cylinder: a trivial stabilizer gives a plane, while a rank-one stabilizer gives a cylinder. The rotating-cylinder sketch is a qualitative illustration and does not by itself establish an inviscid or viscous Taylor--Couette instability theorem.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""The boundary annulus is distinguished from a general noncompact homogeneous orbit. No viscosity, rotation rate, onset threshold or boundary-value problem is recoverable from the sketch.""")
item(76,'P000121','remark','The everywhere-collinear or Beltrami exception',r"""If velocity and vorticity are everywhere collinear, the notebook asserts that examples need not have the stated simple orbit structure, without giving an explicit example.""",r"""If \(v\times\omega\equiv0\), the Bernoulli function is constant on a connected domain, and the nonconstant-first-integral proof of the torus/annulus structure is unavailable. The notebook does not identify a particular Beltrami field or a specific dynamical obstruction. Arnold's 1966 discussion lists the trigonometric ABC-type field as an example of this exceptional class, but describes complicated streamline topology there as probable, not as proved in that paper.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',fate=r"""The exceptional case is explicitly discussed in Arnold's paper, printed p. 347 and its footnote. The particular counterexample claimed orally is not identifiable from the notebook, and the paper's speculative language is not upgraded to a proof. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, this occurrence identifies the limitation of the regular Bernoulli-level theorem but not a precise open problem or a verified historical example. No general Beltrami-flow conjecture is imported.""")
ev('Arnold topology stationary ideal fluid analytic torus annulus theorem1965 1966','https://www.numdam.org/article/AIF_1966__16_1_319_0.pdf','read the result','Exact expanded theorem and proof read. Conditions: connected compact real-analytic domain and boundary, analytic steady velocity up to boundary, nonidentically collinear velocity/curl. Critical Bernoulli values and boundary critical values excluded. Independent rank two on compact regular level yields torus; boundary case yields annulus. Paper cites earlier1965 announcement.','Theorem7 pp.346-348; equations(94), proof pp.347-348; bibliography[4] p.360','76')
ev('Arnold Beltrami exceptional case ABC proof status1966','https://www.numdam.org/article/AIF_1966__16_1_319_0.pdf','read the result','The source calls complicated Beltrami streamline topology probable; no proof of chaos or exact counterexample to simple topology is certified from that sentence.','Printed p.347, remark and footnote11','76')
save()

# 77. Equations, stability sketches, and invariant metrics.
talk(77,'1965-12-13','V. I. Arnold','P000122-P000123','Ideal fluids and the geometry of invariant metrics',r"""The dated Arnold heading on P000122 starts this record. Newton's law and the incompressibility constraint lead to the Euler/Navier--Stokes equations; the discussion then sketches viscosity-dependent stability and gives the Lie-group interpretation of rigid-body and fluid motion. P000123 ends with the inertia operator, the bilinear adjoint \(B\), and a tangent/cotangent translation diagram. The actual Euler--Arnold evolution formula begins on the next page and belongs to the following record, not this one. Yudovich's named two-dimensional result is identified with his 1963 original paper. \citep{yudovich1963nonstationary,arnold1966geometrie}""")
item(77,'P000122','definition','Material acceleration and the incompressible equations',r"""\(DV/Dt=\partial_tv+v\partial_xv\), force consists of pressure, an external term and \(\nu\Delta v\), and \(\operatorname{div}v=0\). The notes call the system a dynamical system on fields satisfying boundary conditions.""",r"""For unit constant density in a smooth Euclidean domain, the material acceleration is \(\partial_tv+(v\cdot\nabla)v\). The incompressible Navier--Stokes equations are \(\partial_tv+(v\cdot\nabla)v=-\nabla p+f+\nu\Delta v\), \(\nabla\cdot v=0\); Euler is the inviscid case \(\nu=0\). Pressure enforces the constraint and is determined only up to a function of time. The boundary condition must be specified: impermeability \(v\cdot n=0\) is natural for Euler, while no-slip is a separate viscous condition. On a Riemannian manifold the advective term is \(\nabla_vv\), and a viscous vector Laplacian requires its own geometric convention.""",['yudovich1963nonstationary','arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The repeated force symbol in the source is separated into total force and prescribed external force. Potential external forces may be absorbed into pressure. No boundary condition or global solution theory follows merely from the dynamical-system terminology.""")
item(77,'P000122','theorem','Yudovich global two-dimensional existence and uniqueness',r"""The notes mention short-time existence and, in two dimensions, existence and uniqueness, with the explicit parenthesis Yudovich.""",r"""A precise unforced specialization is: on a bounded simply connected planar domain with \(C^2\) boundary, an initial divergence-free velocity tangent to the boundary with bounded vorticity determines a unique global Euler solution in the class of velocities with bounded vorticity. Smooth initial data yield the corresponding classical solution. The historical local classical three-dimensional result and the two-dimensional global bounded-vorticity result are different theorems; the Yudovich citation concerns inviscid Euler, not an assertion that viscosity is present.""",['yudovich1963nonstationary'],area='hydrodynamics and PDE',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',fate=r"""Yudovich's 1963 original is opened and read: Theorem 5.1 gives uniqueness/existence and Theorem 6.1 summarizes the velocity/pressure solution with bounded initial vorticity. The simply connected unforced version avoids the additional circulation data treated in his multiply connected discussion. \citep{yudovich1963nonstationary}""",reconstruction=r"""The function class, domain regularity and inviscid specialization are supplied. The short-time three-dimensional statement is acknowledged in the original introduction; its older proofs are not represented as read here.""")
item(77,'P000122','claim','Large-viscosity convergence to one equilibrium',r"""Large viscosity is said to give a unique equilibrium, with all trajectories tending to the solution of the associated elliptic equation.""",r"""The statement needs an autonomous forcing, a specified bounded-domain boundary problem, a solution class, and a quantitative smallness condition relative to viscosity. One sufficient conditional criterion is the following: for a no-slip steady solution \(u_*\) in a bounded domain with Poincare constant \(\lambda_1>0\), if \(\|\nabla u_*\|_{L^\infty}<\nu\lambda_1\), every sufficiently regular solution with the same forcing obeys \[\|v(t)-u_*\|_2^2\le e^{-2(\nu\lambda_1-\|\nabla u_*\|_\infty)t}\|v(0)-u_*\|_2^2.\] This is a stability criterion for an existing equilibrium; it does not by itself prove its existence as viscosity varies.""",['yudovich1963nonstationary'],area='hydrodynamics and PDE',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',development='annotated',fate=r"""No exact large-viscosity theorem is identified from this sentence. The cited Yudovich article is the independently identified inviscid result nearby, not a source for the viscous assertion. The displayed conditional estimate is proved here from the difference energy identity.""",reconstruction=r"""\ours{} If \(w=v-u_*\), cancellation of the divergence-free transport terms yields \(\frac12\frac d{dt}\|w\|_2^2+\nu\|\nabla w\|_2^2=-\int(w\cdot\nabla)u_*\cdot w\); the estimate follows by Poincare and Gronwall. This explicitly bounded reconstruction is not silently substituted for the source's missing existence theorem.""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, the supplied sufficient criterion is proved, while the exact theorem intended by the unqualified large-viscosity sentence remains unidentified. No generic Navier--Stokes open problem is assigned to it.""")
item(77,'P000122','question','What happens as viscosity decreases?',r"""The question is followed by a spectral sketch and the statement that eigenvalues of the variational equation move to the right when viscosity decreases.""",r"""For a specified branch \(u_\nu\) of steady solutions, one can study the spectrum of the linearized operator \(L_\nu w=-P((u_\nu\cdot\nabla)w+(w\cdot\nabla)u_\nu)+\nu P\Delta w\) on a chosen divergence-free space, with boundary conditions and projection \(P\). Decreasing \(\nu\) does not by itself give a general monotonicity theorem for each eigenvalue of this nonselfadjoint family; both the base flow and its operator domain matter. The notebook gives neither a base-flow family nor a threshold question.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamic stability',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='low',fate=r"""Arnold's paper supplies the inviscid variational framework but does not identify the unspecified viscous spectral family in this sketch. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""",reconstruction=r"""The operator formula is a conventional precise setup, not a recovered source formula. A generic instability diagram cannot be promoted to a definite new research problem.""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, no well-posed viscosity-dependent spectral problem is recoverable from the passage. Its class F records that limitation and does not assert absence of later stability theory.""")
item(77,'P000123','claim','Hamiltonian spectral symmetry in the inviscid limit',r"""At \(\nu=0\) the spectrum is said to be symmetric about both axes, with a partly unclear Hamiltonian parenthesis.""",r"""For a real finite-dimensional Hamiltonian linearization \(A=JH\), where \(J\) is invertible skew-symmetric and \(H\) symmetric, eigenvalues occur with their algebraic multiplicities in the set \(\lambda,-\lambda,\overline\lambda,-\overline\lambda\). Reality gives conjugation and the Hamiltonian identity gives sign symmetry. For Euler's infinite-dimensional Hamiltonian/Poisson operator, an analogous assertion requires a specified symplectic leaf, operator domains and a distinction between point and continuous spectrum; the finite-dimensional matrix statement alone is not a complete theorem about the PDE spectrum.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamic stability',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r"""\ours{} The matrix assertion follows because \(A^T=-HJ\) is similar to \(-A\), while a real matrix has conjugate-symmetric spectrum. The notebook's broad PDE statement is restricted to a justified conditional analogue.""")
item(77,'P000123','claim','The unreadable conditional spectral-stability theorem',r"""A conditional theorem says that if an inviscid stable flow exists, its spectrum looks like the adjacent unlabelled sketch. A nearby Poiseuille remark calls a flow unstable without specifying geometry or parameters.""",r"""The sketch does not determine a precise spectral theorem. In a finite-dimensional Hamiltonian linearization, Lyapunov stability rules out eigenvalues with positive real part, and sign symmetry then puts eigenvalues on the imaginary axis; this does not by itself imply semisimplicity of a zero eigenvalue or nonlinear stability from spectral information. A Poiseuille-flow assertion would require planar or pipe geometry, boundary conditions, Reynolds number, perturbation space and the intended notion of instability.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamic stability',cl='F',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low',fate=r"""The published energy-based stability criterion is more precise than this sketch and cannot be assumed to be its exact missing content. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""",reconstruction=r"""Only the limited matrix-theoretic implication is supplied as context; neither the shape of the unreadable diagram nor a particular Poiseuille theorem is invented.""",status=r"""As of 9 November 2026, the passage is too incomplete to identify a definite spectral or flow-instability problem. No current open-status claim follows.""")
item(77,'P000123','construction','Inertia operator and the left-invariant Riemannian metric',r"""\(L:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak g^*\) is symmetric positive definite and \(\langle a,b\rangle_g=\langle dL_{g^{-1}}a,dL_{g^{-1}}b\rangle_e\).""",r"""On a finite-dimensional Lie group \(G\), a symmetric positive-definite linear isomorphism \(\mathcal I:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak g^*\) defines \(\langle u,v\rangle_e=(\mathcal Iu)(v)\). Left translation gives a Riemannian metric, with inertia map \(\mathcal I_g=(dL_{g^{-1}})^*\mathcal I\,dL_{g^{-1}}:T_gG\to T_g^*G\). In infinite dimensions a weak positive metric need not identify the tangent space with its full continuous dual; that extra functional-analytic conclusion is not automatic.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The symbol \(\mathcal I\) distinguishes inertia from left translation. The exact tangent/cotangent placement repairs the ambiguity in the lower arrows of the handwritten diagram.""")
item(77,'P000123','theorem','Free rigid-body motion as a geodesic on SO(3)',r"""For \(G=SO(3)\), geodesics describe rotation of a rigid body about a fixed point; the metric is kinetic energy.""",r"""For a rigid body with a fixed point and no applied torque, the positive inertia tensor defines a left-invariant kinetic-energy metric on \(SO(3)\). Its geodesics are precisely the free rotations, with the usual factor \(1/2\) in kinetic energy. An asymmetric top generally has a left-invariant metric that is not bi-invariant; no external potential or torque is included.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',fate=r"""Arnold presents this as the classical Euler rigid-body model motivating the general group construction. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""")
item(77,'P000123','theorem','Euler flow as a geodesic of the right-invariant L2 metric',r"""\(G=\operatorname{SDiff}D\), \(\langle a,b\rangle=\int_D(a,b)\,dx\), and ideal-fluid flows are geodesics; this follows the preceding left-invariant construction without a stated change of convention.""",r"""For an oriented compact Riemannian domain, smooth volume-preserving fluid motions \(\eta_t\) with impermeable boundary are critical points of kinetic action for the right-invariant \(L^2\) metric on volume-preserving diffeomorphisms. The Eulerian velocity \(v=\dot\eta_t\circ\eta_t^{-1}\) satisfies the incompressible Euler equation. Inversion converts right-invariant to left-invariant notation and changes the associated velocity/bracket convention. A rigorous infinite-dimensional geodesic-flow existence theorem needs an appropriate Sobolev manifold and regularity analysis; the smooth formal identification alone does not supply it.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',cl='A',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r"""The physical particle map uses right invariance. The explicit convention change prevents importing the left-invariant sign into Eulerian velocity formulas. The functional-analytic existence question is separated from this variational identity.""",fate=r"""Arnold's 1966 paper, section 10, pp. 344--345, gives the exact right-invariant fluid interpretation and explains the sign change from the earlier left-invariant convention. This is the full post-talk source checked here; its introduction also mentions 1965 announcements, without a priority claim being made for this occurrence. \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""")
item(77,'P000123','construction','The affine group realizes the hyperbolic plane',r"""For \(G=\{x\mapsto ax+b:a>0\}\), the notes identify the resulting dynamics with the geodesic flow on the Lobachevsky plane and ask to inspect the invariant metric.""",r"""Write group coordinates as \((b,a)\) with \(a>0\) and multiplication \((b,a)(b',a')=(b+ab',aa')\). Declaring the dilation and translation generators orthonormal at the identity gives the left-invariant metric \(ds^2=(db^2+da^2)/a^2\). This is the standard upper-half-plane hyperbolic metric of curvature \(-1\), so its geodesic flow realizes the stated example. Different overall metric scales change the curvature normalization.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='Lie groups and Riemannian geometry',development='annotated',match='thematic only',reconstruction=r"""\ours{} The explicit group law, metric and curvature normalization are computed here. The cited Arnold source supplies the invariant-metric framework; this affine example is not asserted to have been located verbatim there.""")
item(77,'P000123','definition','The bilinear metric-adjoint operation B',r"""\(\langle[a,b],c\rangle=\langle b,B(c,a)\rangle\).""",r"""On a finite-dimensional metric Lie algebra, this identity uniquely defines \(B(c,a)=\operatorname{ad}_a^{\dagger}c\), the metric adjoint of \(b\mapsto[a,b]\). It is bilinear and satisfies \(\langle B(c,a),b\rangle+\langle B(c,b),a\rangle=0\). For a weak infinite-dimensional metric, existence of such an adjoint in the chosen smooth Lie algebra must be established separately. This record defines \(B\); its evolution equation appears in the following record.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',fate=r"""Arnold's equation (10), printed p. 322, is an exact textual match, with the immediate antisymmetry identity (11). \citep{arnold1966geometrie}""",reconstruction=r"""The placement of \(c,a\) in \(B\) is checked against both scan and original publication; it is not replaced by a differently ordered adjoint convention.""")
item(77,'P000123','construction','Left and right trivializations of tangent and cotangent vectors',r"""The lower diagram places \(T_gG\) between two copies of the Lie algebra, with downward inertia maps and dual arrows in the bottom row; the last star and one lower arrow are overwritten.""",r"""For a tangent velocity \(\dot g\), the left and right trivializations are \(u=dL_{g^{-1}}\dot g\) and \(w=dR_{g^{-1}}\dot g\). Their dual maps send Lie-algebra covectors to covectors at \(g\), with arrows contravariant to tangent maps. The left-invariant inertia square commutes by \(\mathcal I_g=(dL_{g^{-1}})^*\mathcal I\,dL_{g^{-1}}\). The same fixed \(\mathcal I\) in a right-hand square would require a right-invariant metric, unless an appropriate transformed inertia is inserted. The overwritten terminal node is read mathematically as a dual algebra, not as evidence for a bidual identification.""",['arnold1966geometrie'],area='hydrodynamics and geometric mechanics',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r"""Only the square justified by the stated left-invariant metric is asserted to commute. The diagram is not treated as evidence that the metric is automatically bi-invariant.""")
